    "long_instructions": "**☑️ Do This:**\r\n- Review the training data in `notes.py`.\r\n  - Each `note` is a dictionary with two keys: `\"frequency\"` and `\"volume\"`.\r\n- Complete `predict_instrument`. It should predict the instrument playing each `note` by returning either `\"Violin\"` or `\"Cello\"`. Currently, it only returns `\"Cello\"`.\r\n- Run the program to test `predict_instrument`. You’ll see your classifier’s **accuracy** on a set of **test data**.\r\n- Update `predict_instrument` to try to improve it.\r\n- Test again, update again, and repeat until you arrive at the best test result you can.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nRemember that you can access a dictionary’s values by using its keys. For example, to access a note’s frequency, you can use:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nnote[\"frequency\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\nTry creating a classifier based on the note’s frequency.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nif note[\"frequency\"] > 200:\r\n    ...\r\nelse:\r\n    ...\r\n```",
